1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,160
 So now we're going to wrap up and talk a little bit about how strings and lists are related.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:11,160
 They're sort of related in that they both have zero-based things and we use the square bracket operator to do various things.

3
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:16,760
 But there's a lot of situations where we're looking at our data and we're combining the use of lists and strings.

4
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:20,600
 So let me show you the first thing, probably the coolest thing.

5
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,480
 We're going to use it a lot, the rest of the class, and that is the split function.

6
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:27,960
 So let's take a string, we've got ABC here, it's with three words.

7
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:31,560
 What we're interested in in the fact is that there's spaces in this word.

8
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:37,640
 And what split does is says, you know, I'm going to look through this thing, I'm going to find this, and I'm going to break this into pieces.

9
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:41,160
 And I'm going to return you a list of the separate individual pieces.

10
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:46,000
 So read, look for blanks, and break it in pieces, and give me back the pieces.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,680
 So I'll print these out and now you see that it's a list with three items, with three words.

12
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,600
 The spaces are gone, but it's given it to us.

13
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:56,800
 So it's like, split this into words, please, and give me the individual words.

14
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,400
 And give me a list of individual words, rather than a big long string with spaces in the middle of it.

15
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:06,960
 And that is a quick way to go from a line, and it's really common.

16
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:10,480
 A lot of things are gone, like go get the second thing, or the third thing, or whatever.

17
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:13,360
 So the split's really nice, because then you can just grab stuff.

18
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,200
 And so you say, oh, how many things did I get?

19
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:15,960
 Well, I got three.

20
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:18,000
 The len function tells us that.

21
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,480
 And I can print the first word I got, which is, and with the sub zero.

22
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:26,960
 And that'll be like with, will be the first word, because that's the sub zero position.

23
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:31,320
 So I read something, I split it, I can say there's three things,

24
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:35,240
 and I can look at stuff, the first word, basically, without really knowing much.

25
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:42,000
 Now if you remember earlier, and we'll see this, we used find and slicing to do a similar kind of thing.

26
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,720
 But people tend to prefer the split.

27
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:50,560
 And you can, oops, go back.

28
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:54,600
 You can also then loop through them.

29
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:59,760
 So you can split these things into stuff as a word, and then go through with w.

30
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:05,520
 And then it's going to go through, w is going to take the successive with three words.

31
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:10,240
 And so you can make a loop by reading some data, splitting it, and then writing a for loop.

32
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:14,000
 And then it's effectively going through the words in that line of data.

33
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,960
 And so that's a really powerful concept that we'll use in a lot of the programs that we're going to write.

34
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:23,840
 Just a couple of bits about this and how it works.

35
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:30,680
 Split with no parameters here, it looks for spaces, but it also treats a bunch of spaces as a single space.

36
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,680
 And so it's pretty smart about that.

37
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:39,040
 And so even though this has a lot of spaces between lot and of, you only see lot of, all the spaces are gone.

38
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:41,120
 It does something special about spaces.

39
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:49,160
 It's really white space, so tabs or new lines or other characters would also qualify in split, basically.

40
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:54,160
 Now, you don't always have to split based on spaces, and a lot of data that you're going to run into,

41
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,680
 you're going to want to split on something else.

42
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:01,400
 And so here's some data that looks like we're using colons to separate the first, second, and third piece.

43
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,800
 Now if you just call split, split's looking for spaces.

44
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:11,120
 And so split gives you back a list of the things broken apart with spaces, but there's not a single space in that line.

45
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:16,240
 And so we get a list, see it's a list, but there's only one item, and the semicolons are sitting there.

46
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:19,520
 Split doesn't go like, "Oh, this looks like it should be semicolons."

47
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:25,080
 Split's job is to use spaces and split the string based on spaces, okay?

48
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:32,240
 But given that this is something we like to do, you can tell split what character you'd actually like to split on.

49
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:35,800
 Now it's not quite as clever when splitting on something other than spaces.

50
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:42,840
 It doesn't understand that if there's a bunch of semicolons in a row, it still thinks of those as points to split.

51
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:47,200
 But in this particular case where there's no spaces, and it's going to split that.

52
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:54,360
 So it says split this based on the semicolon, instead of being based on the space.

53
00:03:54,360 --> 00:04:01,840
 And so if you take a look at what comes out of this, we split on semicolon, now we have a three-item list, and we get first, second, and third.

54
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:09,240
 And a lot of your data comes out of some logging system or some router status updates, who knows what you're looking at.

55
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:17,120
 But the delimiter is often something other than space, and you can do that with split.

56
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:21,640
 So this is a useful thing when parsing things like our email address, right?

57
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:28,600
 We wanted to get things like the email address, this second piece, off of the line.

58
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:33,320
 And so we can use split to take advantage of this.

59
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:39,600
 And so here's a little loop that's just going to print out not the email addresses, but instead, the day of the week.

60
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:42,160
 We're going to print the day of the week out for all these things.

61
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:43,040
 How do we do that?

62
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:56,520
 Well, we can observe really quickly that if we split based on spaces, it's the 0, 1, 2, it's the 2 position.

63
00:04:56,520 --> 00:05:05,240
 So we can quickly write a bit of code that opens the file, then loops through the lines, we do this all the time now.

64
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:08,240
 A strip takes off the end of the new lines.

65
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:10,920
 We can check to see if it starts with from space, right?

66
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:16,320
 From space is our key, so we're ignoring all of the lines that don't start with from space.

67
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:22,040
 But then we find a line that starts with from space, and we split it, and then we just print out the second word.

68
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:32,260
 And so we get the second word of the lines that start with from, and that's how this thing works.

69
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:40,800
 Now, sometimes we want to dig into deeper, and we will take something, split it, and then split another piece of it again with a different delimiter.

70
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:46,920
 So let's just say that the thing that we want to achieve is getting the part after the @ sign for email addresses.

71
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:52,760
 And we did this with, again, find and pos and stuff like that, but you can use split to do this as well.

72
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:56,760
 So the first thing we're going to do is we're going to take this line, we're going to split it based on spaces, right?

73
00:05:56,760 --> 00:06:04,520
 Chop, chop, chop, chop, chop, and the fact that there's an extra space there doesn't matter, split happily just like zooms through that.

74
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:12,840
 And then words sub 1, 0, 1, 2, words sub 1 is this email address, so we'll put that in a variable called email.

75
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:20,160
 And so email will be a string that's just this, so in two lines we've pulled out the second address into a variable.

76
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:24,320
 Then what we're going to do is we're going to re-split that.

77
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:29,720
 We're going to take this string we've got and split it based on @ sign, because we know it's an email address.

78
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:39,280
 So we get a new set of pieces, the first part is the person's name, and the second part is the host name that their email is hosted on.

79
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:49,720
 And then what we can do then is we just happen to know that this is the zero item and this is the one item, so we can get at that.

80
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:58,160
 So the interesting thing of going here, if you think back to how we did this before with find and pos and all that stuff, it's really a lot cleaner.

81
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:04,880
 And we don't, for me, I can look at this after you understand it and it's easy for me to understand that it's correct.

82
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:11,000
 Whereas that pos stuff, you've got to add one and start the second find after, just remember that.

83
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:18,560
 And this is a lot cleaner way, and this is a more typical way of pulling this kind of information out of a line.

84
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:23,360
 So in this chapter we've talked about lists, we've talked about the concept of collections, that's our first data structure.

85
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:28,320
 We're not just doing algorithms, we kind of know algorithms now, but now we're going to do data structures.

86
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:32,240
 And this chapter and the next two chapters are our foundational data structures.

87
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:38,480
 And then we'll, like everything, we'll make more complex data structures by composing those data structures together.

88
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:47,040
 We've looked at how strings and lists connect together and how split works, and these are all really powerful tools that we're going to use going forward.

